{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789463222,"nonce":"mxXlGRN/LNeSmnHKtYW3RM57Sag=","title":"A closure in a static method reads the class's static fields as null","message":"```wren\nclass A {\n  static go() {\n    __x = 1\n    var f = Fiber.new { System.print(\"in fiber x=%(__x)\") }\n    f.call()\n    var g = Fn.new { System.print(\"in fn x=%(__x)\") }\n    g.call()\n  }\n}\nA.go()\n```\n\nprints `in fiber x=null` and `in fn x=null` in every mode (interpreter, tiered, WLIFT_KRIO_FIBER=0); wren_c prints 1 for both, since a nested function in a method reaches the class through the enclosing `this`. The same read outside the closure is fine.\n\nConsequence: a `Thread.create { __lock.release() }` or `Fiber.spawn { ... __log.add(x) }` inside a static method calls a method on null. Under the scheduler the task ends at once with no error reported through `error_fn` (the fiber is stepped with `is_try` set), so a `wait()` on the lock parks forever: the silent half is a second defect worth a look. Found writing caribou's one-world test; its fixture uses module variables instead.","files":null}]}